<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext, and where the rule that limits it actually lives (§8).

  §8 allows plain http for a local model server such as Ollama or LM Studio,
  "with a prominent warning and narrowly scoped Android network-security
  configuration". The warning is on the settings screen. The narrow scope is
  NOT here, and it cannot be:

  This file lists hosts. It has no way to express "any address on the user's own
  network", and the user's LAN address — 192.168.1.42, or whatever their router
  handed out — is not knowable when the app is built. An allowlist of literal
  hosts would mean a user running Ollama on their desktop simply cannot reach
  it, which is the case §8 names first.

  So the platform permits cleartext and EndpointPolicy decides. It refuses
  http:// unless the user has switched it on AND the address is a numeric
  address on a private network. A public host over http is refused whatever this
  file says.

  That moves a platform guarantee into application code, which is only safe if
  the application code is the only door. CleartextGuardTest keeps it that way:
  exactly one module depends on an HTTP client, exactly one file makes calls
  with it, and that file consults EndpointPolicy before every request. If any of
  those three stops being true, this file has quietly become the app's whole
  cleartext policy — and it does not have one.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <!--
              System CAs only. User-installed certificates are deliberately not
              trusted: that is the default above API 24, and restating it here
              means a later edit to this file has to remove a line rather than
              forget one. It also keeps a self-signed local server from being a
              supported setup, which would be the other way people ask for LAN
              support and a much worse one.
            -->
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
